      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017, The American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology.",
        "AbstractText": "Elevated plasma low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol is the dominant risk factor for the development of atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease. Deficiency in the LDL receptor (LDLR) is a major cause of familial hypercholesterolemia in humans, and the LDLR knockout mouse is a major animal model of atherosclerosis. Here we report the generation and characterization of an ldlr mutant zebrafish as a new animal model to study hypercholesterolemia and vascular lipid accumulation, an early event in the development of human atherosclerosis. The ldlr mutant zebrafish were characterized by activated Srebp2 pathway and developed moderate hypercholesterolemia when fed normal diet. However, a short-term, 5-day feeding of ldlr mutant larvae with a high-cholesterol diet (HCD) resulted in exacerbated hypercholesterolemia and accumulation of vascular lipid deposits. Lomitapide, an inhibitor of apoB lipoprotein secretion, but not the antioxidant probucol, significantly reduced accumulation of vascular lipid deposits in HCD fed ldlr mutant larvae. Furthermore, ldlr mutants were defective in hepatic clearance of LPS, resulting in reduced survival. Taken together, our data suggest that ldlr knockout zebrafish is a versatile model to study the function of the LDL receptor, hypercholesterolemia and related vascular pathology in the context of early atherosclerosis."
      },
